+
+### [Windowed Sinc Interpolation](18705-windowed-sinc-interpolation.md)
+Approximates a continuous volume function by convolving discrete voxel samples with a truncated sinc kernel modulated by a window function, primarily to reduce aliasing artifacts inherent in ideal sinc interpolation while maintaining high frequency response. Distinguished from its siblings by its explicit support for anisotropic voxel resampling via a directional blur factor, making it particularly suitable for converting anisotropic medical imaging data (e.g., breast tomosynthesis) into isotropic grids for volume rendering. Chosen when anisotropic data requires isotropic resampling.
 
 ## Background
-Kernel-based resampling is a fundamental technique in digital signal processing and computer graphics, rooted in the Whittaker–Shannon interpolation formula which states that a band-limited signal can be perfectly reconstructed from its samples using a sinc kernel . In volume rendering, exact sinc reconstruction is computationally prohibitive due to its infinite support, leading to the development of truncated and windowed kernels (such as Lanczos) and polynomial approximations (such as B-splines) . The method is widely used in direct volume ray casting to ensure smooth visual transitions and accurate gradient estimation for lighting calculations.
